fix(http): trust X-Forwarded-For only from a configured proxy

A client could escape its rate limit entirely, and could spend another
client's budget, by setting a request header.

The defect
  The limiter counted requests against the first entry of
  X-Forwarded-For whenever the header was present. That header is set by
  the caller, so:
    * rotating it across requests put every request in a fresh bucket and
      the limit was never reached - the control was effectively absent
      while still appearing to work;
    * naming somebody else's address consumed that person's budget, so a
      targeted denial of service cost the attacker nothing.

The correction
  The header is read only when the immediate peer is listed in the new
  TRUSTED_PROXIES setting, which means the value was appended by
  infrastructure the deployment controls. The chain is then read from the
  right-hand end, because a client can prepend entries of its own but
  cannot remove the one the last trusted hop appended.

  TRUSTED_PROXIES defaults to empty, so a direct deployment ignores the
  header and shares one budget. That is the safe failure mode: the
  deployment is less convenient than intended, not less protected.

Why the tests did not catch it
  The suite connects directly, and the previous rate-limit cases never
  sent the header, so the untrusted path was never exercised. Three cases
  are added that fail against the previous implementation: spoofing does
  not reset the budget, naming a victim does not consume the victim's
  budget, and a prepended entry does not create a fresh bucket.

Refs: ISYS3001 A2 - configuration management (ULO2)